      "description": "5622 Apprenticeship Skills Coach - Jobs at Greenwich Skip to main content Jobs home Login Register Current vacancies Staff Visa Compliance Working at Greenwich Search jobs Equality, diversity and inclusion Sustainability Frequently asked questions Applying Contact us Terms and conditions Cookies Accessibility Faculties Portal Jobs home Login Register More ▼ Cookies This site requires the use of cookies as defined by our Terms and Conditions . \n        We have provided a detailed description of how cookies work and are used on the site . \n        To accept cookies, please click the \"Accept Cookies\" button. Accept cookies Apprenticeship Skills Coach FES Professional Services Location: Greenwich Campus Salary: £39,560 to £45,641\n            plus £5586 London weighting per annum Contract Type: Fixed Term\n             - Maternity Cover between 07/12/2026 and 31/12/2027 Release Date: Tuesday 13 October 2026 Closing Date: Thursday 22 October 2026 Interview Date: To be confirmed Reference: 5622 On the 1 August 2026, the University of Greenwich and the University of Kent formed a pioneering new multi-university group called London and South-East (LASE) University Group. Should you join us, you will be employed by LASE but will work as part of the University of Greenwich academic division, contributing to its distinct strengths, community and identity while also being part of a wider group that brings together the scale, resilience and shared ambition of both universities. Through LASE we aim to deliver outstanding education, impactful research, and meaningful regional and global impact. The University of Greenwich is seeking to expand its Apprenticeship Skills Coach provision within the Faculty of Engineering and Science. We are looking for a proactive and engaging individual with excellent interpersonal skills to support apprentices and trainee professionals across a range of disciplines. As an Apprenticeship Skills Coach, you will work closely with apprentices, employers and academic colleagues to monitor and review progress against apprenticeship standards, supporting learners to successfully achieve their apprenticeship and End Point Assessment. You will act as a key point of contact for apprentices, providing information, advice and guidance throughout their apprenticeship journey. The role involves maintaining strong relationships with employers and other stakeholders, monitoring learner progress, and contributing to the continued growth and success of our apprenticeship provision. Through regular progress reviews with apprentices and their line managers, you will adopt a coaching approach to help learners develop and apply their knowledge, skills and behaviours within the workplace, ensuring they remain on track to achieve and complete their apprenticeship on time. The successful candidate will possess excellent organisational, administrative and digital skills, together with a flexible, learner-focused approach and a commitment to supporting positive learner outcomes. Applicants must have knowledge and understanding of higher and degree apprenticeship programmes, together with experience in a similar apprenticeship, coaching, training or learner support role. Experience of supporting learners in a work-based learning environment and maintaining effective employer relationships would be advantageous. The role is within the professional services team in the Faculty of Engineering and Sciences based between the Medway Campus in Chatham Maritime, and the Greenwich Campus in Greenwich, London. The role will require collaborative work with colleagues in the Faculty and across other centralised University teams, and is matrix managed by the Head of Apprenticeships. We offer a friendly and supportive working environment with plenty of opportunities to develop within the role and become more involved in the wider university community. As part of our commitment to safeguarding and safer recruitment practices, appointment to this role is subject to an enhanced Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check, and an online search may be conducted on shortlisted candidates in line with our employment policies. For an informal discussion about the post please contact Jacklyn Tubb, Head of Business Operations on J.Edwards-Tubb@gre.ac.uk Should you have any queries please contact the People Directorate Team on peopleoperationsdmin@gre.ac.uk Further details of the fantastic benefits and what we offer can be found here: Our benefits - what we offer | Jobs and careers | University of Greenwich and information on the university’s working principles: Working Principles and Guidelines | Documents | University of Greenwich To apply, please visit the University of Greenwich vacancies page and complete all details, including the supporting statements (please note attached CVs and personal statements will not be seen by the recruitment panel at the application stage): Current Vacancies - Jobs at Greenwich Employment Visa: Under the UK Government Points Based System scheme this role does / does not meet the criteria to be sponsored for a Skilled Worker Route application. We are looking for people who can help us deliver our mission of transforming lives through inspired teaching and research, through our values. Academic Career Pathways The University is committed to supporting and promoting the welfare of staff, students (including apprentices) and visitors and is committed to the provision of a safe environment conducive to work, study and the enjoyment of a positive experience for all members of its community. As part of providing a safe environment in which to work and study, and protecting the public, the University has a Safeguarding Policy . We are committed to building a strong, diverse workforce that reflects the communities we serve. We particularly encourage applications from [protected characteristic(s)], who are currently under-represented within the University of Greenwich at these levels/within these areas. We do this through taking positive action such as encouraging applications from Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic, care leavers, disabled and LGBT+ people. As part of our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion, Time to Change Employer Pledge / Mentally Healthy Universities , we are committed to promoting and supporting the physical and mental health of all our staff, and removing barriers to improve inclusion. We encourage applicants to disclose experience of mental health problems so we can support them fully during our recruitment process and make any necessary reasonable adjustments. Any information disclosed will be kept confidential and separate from the job application form. We are making significant strides to understand and continuously improve our employees’ experience and we are committed to implementing progressive diversity talent management. Email details to a friend Apply online Further details: Job Description & Person Specification Share: View All Vacancies In this section Current vacancies Staff Visa Compliance Working at Greenwich Search jobs Equality, diversity and inclusion Sustainability Frequently asked questions Applying Find jobs Jobs by email Jobs by RSS Search jobs: Search Advanced job search Login Please note: Javascript must be enabled to use this website. Email / Username: Password: I accept cookies Login Forgotten details Register Contact us | Terms and conditions | Cookies | Accessibility © University of Greenwich"
    },
    "https://jobs.gre.ac.uk/vacancy.aspx?ref=5618": {
      "title": "5618 Research Entomologist in Chemical Ecology",
      "description": "5618 Research Entomologist in Chemical Ecology - Jobs at Greenwich Skip to main content Jobs home Login Register Current vacancies Staff Visa Compliance Working at Greenwich Search jobs Equality, diversity and inclusion Sustainability Frequently asked questions Applying Contact us Terms and conditions Cookies Accessibility Faculties Portal Jobs home Login Register More ▼ Cookies This site requires the use of cookies as defined by our Terms and Conditions . \n        We have provided a detailed description of how cookies work and are used on the site . \n        To accept cookies, please click the \"Accept Cookies\" button. Accept cookies Research Entomologist in Chemical Ecology Agriculture, Health and Environment Location: Medway Campus Salary: £39,560 to £45,641\n            per annum Contract Type: Permanent Release Date: Wednesday 21 October 2026 Closing Date: Wednesday 04 November 2026 Interview Date: To be confirmed Reference: 5618 On the 1 August 2026, the University of Greenwich and the University of Kent formed a pioneering new multi-university group called London and South-East (LASE) University Group. Should you join us, you will be employed by LASE but will work as part of the University of Greenwich academic division, contributing to its distinct strengths, community and identity while also being part of a wider group that brings together the scale, resilience and shared ambition of both universities. Through LASE we aim to deliver outstanding education, impactful research, and meaningful regional and global impact. The Natural Resources Institute is a specialist research, knowledge exchange and postgraduate training unit based at the University of Greenwich’s Medway Campus. We aspire to be a global leader in collaborative research, knowledge exchange and learning to co-create a just, healthy, and sustainable future for all. We do this through transforming lives through research, innovation, and learning. The NRI has an international footprint and is home to over 80 international scientists and social scientists. We have made award-winning discoveries collaborating with partners across sectors and disciplines to co-create environmental resilience, sustainable agriculture, health, and food security. NRI works towards a just world free from disease, hunger, poverty, and violence. We are an inclusive, collaborative, and flexible research and learning institute. The Institute has a strong history of innovative research to address global challenges related to agriculture and food security. The unique diversity of disciplines across NRI’s three departments provides opportunities for transdisciplinary and systems research relevant to tackling the complexity of contemporary and emerging global agriculture, biodiversity, food security, food system and one-health challenges. An opportunity has arisen for a research entomologist to join the Agriculture, Health and Environment department. The successful applicant will carry out behavioural bioassays and electrophysiological assays on arthropods as part of our work on beneficial insects and sustainable control of pests and diseases of crops, livestock, and people. The post-holder’s responsibilities will include carrying out bioassays with terrestrial arthropods to measure responses to chemical and other stimuli. This will include electrophysiological assays (electroantennography, single sensillum recording) and behavioural bioassays in olfactometers and wind tunnels. The post-holder will have the opportunity to use apparatus for automated 2D- and 3D- tracking of insect behaviour. They will also be expected to work with the technical team to maintain colonies of arthropods in our insectaries and to conduct field work in the UK and for short periods abroad. The post-holder will be expected to contribute to the preparation of research outputs including published papers, and applications for future funding to continue research. There will be opportunities for the post-holder to undertake teaching at postgraduate and undergraduate level. Candidates must have a good theoretical and practical background in behavioural entomology or arthropod chemical ecology as demonstrated through a relevant PhD. Specific training in use of bioassay equipment will be provided. For an informal discussion about the post please contact Dr Daniel Bray at d.bray@greenwich.ac.uk Should you have any recruitment related queries please contact the People Directorate Operations Team on peopleoperationsfes@greenwich.ac.uk Further details of the fantastic benefits and what we offer can be found here: Our benefits - what we offer | Jobs and careers | University of Greenwich and information on the University’s working principles: Working Principles and Guidelines | Documents | University of Greenwich To apply, please visit the University of Greenwich vacancies page and complete all details, including the supporting statements (please note attached CVs and personal statements will not be seen by the recruitment panel at the application stage). Current Vacancies - Jobs at Greenwich Academic Career Pathways We are committed to building a strong, diverse workforce that reflects the communities we serve. We particularly encourage applications from [protected characteristic(s)], who are currently under-represented within the University of Greenwich at these levels/within these areas. We do this through taking positive action such as encouraging applications from Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic, disabled and LGBT+ people. As part of our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion, Time to Change Employer Pledge / Mentally Healthy Universities , we are committed to promoting and supporting the physical and mental health of all our staff, and removing barriers to improve inclusion. We encourage applicants to disclose experience of mental health problems so we can support them fully during our recruitment process and make any necessary reasonable adjustments. Any information disclosed will be kept confidential and separate from the job application form. We are making significant strides to understand and continuously improve our employees’ experience and we are committed to implementing progressive diversity talent management. Email details to a friend Apply online Further details: Job Description & Person Specification Share: View All Vacancies In this section Current vacancies Staff Visa Compliance Working at Greenwich Search jobs Equality, diversity and inclusion Sustainability Frequently asked questions Applying Find jobs Jobs by email Jobs by RSS Search jobs: Search Advanced job search Login Please note: Javascript must be enabled to use this website. Email / Username: Password: I accept cookies Login Forgotten details Register Contact us | Terms and conditions | Cookies | Accessibility © University of Greenwich"
    },
    "https://jobs.gre.ac.uk/vacancy.aspx?ref=5616": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "5616 Research Fellow - Indigenous Peoples' Data Governance - Jobs at Greenwich Skip to main content Jobs home Login Register Current vacancies Staff Visa Compliance Working at Greenwich Search jobs Equality, diversity and inclusion Sustainability Frequently asked questions Applying Contact us Terms and conditions Cookies Accessibility Faculties Portal Jobs home Login Register Cookies This site requires the use of cookies as defined by our Terms and Conditions . \r\n        We have provided a detailed description of how cookies work and are used on the site . \r\n        To accept cookies, please click the \"Accept Cookies\" button. Accept Cookies Research Fellow - Indigenous Peoples' Data Governance Livelihoods & Institutions Location: Medway Campus Salary: £39,560 to £45,641\r\n            per annum Contract Type: Fixed Term\r\n             - between 01/01/2027 and 31/07/2029 Release Date: Wednesday 21 October 2026 Closing Date: Wednesday 04 November 2026 Interview Date: To be confirmed Reference: 5616 On 1st August 2026, The University of Greenwich and the University of Kent formed a pioneering new multi-university group called London and South- East (LASE) University Group. Should you join us, you will be employed by LASE but will work as part of the University of Greenwich academic division, contributing to its distinct strengths, community and identity while also being part of a wider group that brings together the scale, resilience and shared ambition of both universities. Through LASE we aim to deliver outstanding education, impactful research, and meaningful regional and global impact. The Natural Resources Institute (NRI), within the Faculty of Engineering and Science at the University of Greenwich, undertakes interdisciplinary research addressing major global challenges in food systems, agriculture, health, livelihoods and sustainable development. The post will be based in NRI’s Livelihoods and Institutions Department and will form part of IP-STATS, a five-year ERC-funded project advancing epistemically diverse quantitative research on Indigenous Peoples’ food systems. We are seeking a Research Fellow specialising in Indigenous Peoples’ data governance. Working with an interdisciplinary and intercultural team and Indigenous Peoples’ organisations and communities, the post-holder will lead and contribute to research on Indigenous data sovereignty, Indigenous-led quantitative methodologies and the governance of food-systems data. Responsibilities include: Co-designing research questions Methods and governance arrangements Reviewing data-governance frameworks and statistical systems Supporting culturally respectful fieldwork and partner engagement Helping develop, pilot and disseminate the IP-STATS toolkit with partners in Ghana, Tanzania, Uganda, Peru, Nepal, India, Sri Lanka and Kyrgyzstan. Essential requirements include long-term lived experience that has developed substantive knowledge and understanding of Indigenous Peoples’ cultures, rights, priorities and contemporary issues, together with the ability to communicate effectively, respectfully and inclusively with Indigenous communities. Experience of collaborative research across cultures and knowledge traditions is also essential. Candidates who self-identify as Indigenous are particularly encouraged to apply. For an informal discussion about the post please contact Pamela Katic at p.g.katic@gre.ac.uk Should you have any recruitment related queries please contact the People Directorate Operations Team on peopleoperationsfes@gre.ac.uk. Further details of the fantastic benefits and what we offer can be found here: Our benefits - what we offer | Jobs and careers | University of Greenwich and information on the university’s working principles: Working Principles and Guidelines | Documents | University of Greenwich To apply, please visit the University of Greenwich vacancies page and complete all details, including the supporting statements (please note attached CVs and personal statements will not be seen by the recruitment panel at the application stage). Current Vacancies - Jobs at Greenwich We are looking for people who can help us deliver our mission of transforming lives through inspired teaching and research, through our values. Academic Career Pathways We are committed to building a strong, diverse workforce that reflects the communities we serve. We particularly encourage applications from [protected characteristic(s)], who are currently under-represented within the University of Greenwich at these levels/within these areas. We do this through taking positive action such as encouraging applications from Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic, disabled and LGBT+ people. As part of our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion, Time to Change Employer Pledge / Mentally Healthy Universities , we are committed to promoting and supporting the physical and mental health of all our staff, and removing barriers to improve inclusion. We encourage applicants to disclose experience of mental health problems so we can support them fully during our recruitment process and make any necessary reasonable adjustments. Any information disclosed will be kept confidential and separate from the job application form. We are making significant strides to understand and continuously improve our employees’ experience and we are committed to implementing progressive diversity talent management. Email details to a friend Apply Online Further details: Job Description & Person Specification Share: View All Vacancies In this section Current vacancies Staff Visa Compliance Working at Greenwich Search jobs Equality, diversity and inclusion Sustainability Frequently asked questions Applying Find jobs Jobs by email Jobs by RSS Search jobs: Search Advanced job search Login Please note: Javascript must be enabled to use this website. Email / Username: Password: I accept cookies Login Forgotten details Register Contact us | Terms and 